# Branch policy: shekyl-core's part

Estate rule [`06-branching`](06-branching.mdc) holds in every Shekyl repository.
This rule holds the part of it that only shekyl-core has: the
consensus-cutover exception to the splitting guidance, and how its CI
tests the merges into `dev`.

## The 5-day / 10-commit guidance has one named exception

The 5-working-day / 10-commit splitting guidance in rule 2 and the
"land on dev within 5 working days" guidance above have a single
ratified exception: PRs that meet all four criteria of
[`07-consensus-atomic-cutovers.mdc`](./07-consensus-atomic-cutovers.mdc)
— consensus-rule boundary, indivisible under flag decomposition,
surface enumerated in advance with grep evidence, and disposition
documented in the PR description with reviewer-map and rollback
procedure. The exception applies only to the PR-splitting guidance;
every other rule in this file (no direct commits to `main`, no
`dev`-merges-into-feature, no force-pushes to `main`/`dev`, no
deletion without confirmation, etc.) is unmodified.

If a PR appears to need the exception, the test is *not* "is this
analogous to LWMA-1 Phase 4" — it is "does this PR meet the four
named criteria in `07-consensus-atomic-cutovers.mdc`?" The exception
is auditable mechanically against the criteria, not by precedent.

## The runs on `dev` test the merge result

A PR merges into `dev` once its own required checks pass; the `dev`
ruleset requires a PR and those checks, and nothing else.
A PR's CI runs on its head merged onto the `dev` of its last push. Two PRs
each green alone can be broken together (#771×#772, #853×#858,
#878×#880), and neither PR's run can see it. The runs on `dev` do: every
gate that checks the whole tree also runs on push to `dev`, with the same
`paths` filter it has on PRs. Consequences:

- **A red run on `dev` is `dev`'s finding, not the last PR's.** The PRs
  were green on their own heads. Each red job opens its own issue,
  assigned to the Foundation's lead, naming the failing tests and the
  merges since the workflow last passed, and the job's next green run
  closes it (`report-dev-red.yml`). Read the diff since the last green run
  on `dev`, find the two changes that disagree, and fix it on a new PR
  before more merges land on top; every open PR that merges `dev` until
  then inherits the red.
- **A run on `dev` that has started finishes** (ruled 2026-11-19, every
  workflow). A workflow's concurrency group is its ref, so every push to
  `dev` shares one. A merge queues behind the running `dev` run, and a
  newer merge replaces the queued one: one runs, one waits, and a burst of
  merges is tested at the commit already running and at its newest. Only
  a pull request's newer push cancels its older run
  (`scripts/ci/check_dev_push_runs_finish.py`, over every workflow that
  pushes on `dev`). The build and Rust scope jobs measure a push from the
  last commit that workflow passed on (`scripts/ci/push_scope_base.sh`),
  not from the previous push, so a replaced or cancelled run's commits stay in scope
  and a docs-only merge cannot skip them. A red names the merges since the
  last green run, not one PR.
- **A gate on a PR's own diff runs on PRs only:** the schema snapshots'
  version-bump pairing (the snapshot tests themselves are in the Rust
  suite) and the benchmark comparator. CodeQL analyses each push to `dev`
  and, weekly, `main`; the depends cross-compiles run per PR and nightly;
  the ProVerif model is not a required check and runs per PR.
- **A draft PR runs the cheap gates only.** The Rust suite, the
  economics sim's narration pins, the OS builds, the benchmark capture
  and the RandomX differential jobs skip a draft, and run when it is
  marked ready and on every push after; their workflows list
  `ready_for_review` (`scripts/ci/check_pr_event_triggers.py`). Ubuntu
  22.04 builds pushes to `dev` and `main` only.
- **Each of the two build workflows has one required check, its pass
  job:** `cli-pass` (`build.yml`) and `rust-pass` (`rust-audit-test.yml`).
  A skipped job counts as a pass to protection, so no job that can be
  skipped is required. The pass job needs every other job and runs
  whatever they did: green when each succeeded or was skipped by scope,
  red when the scope job did not succeed or any job failed or was
  cancelled. On a draft it reports as `<name> (draft)`, so a draft never
  satisfies the requirement, and a PR marked ready waits for its own pass
  job (`scripts/ci/workflow_pass.py`).
- **A PR is a draft while it is worked on, comes out of draft for review,
  and takes `merge-ready` when review and validation are complete**
  (ruled 2026-11-23).
- **`merge-ready` is the owner's "done", enforced** (ruled 2026-11-19).
  Required checks going green is not the end of a PR: other workflows and
  the lane's fleet runs on the same head can still be going. The owning
  lane applies the `merge-ready` label when every check on the current
  head has concluded, not only the required ones, its fleet and local
  runs for that head are done, and its review threads are resolved; the
  handoff to the merger is "labelled merge-ready at <sha>". The required
  status `merge-ready` (`merge-ready.yml`, `scripts/ci/merge_ready.py`)
  is pending until then. The label going on is refused, and taken off
  with a comment naming each reason, while a check runs or is red, a
  review thread is open, or the PR is a draft. A push takes it off, with
  a comment: the label vouches for one head. It is a commit status,
  never a job, so it cannot be skipped into a pass, and no job that
  posts it is named `merge-ready`, since a ruleset matches a check run
  and a status by name alike.
- **The label is a signal to the merger, not a second test run** (ruled
  2026-11-23). Nothing re-runs a test when the label goes on or when a
  merge reaches a labelled PR, and a merge into `dev` leaves the label and
  the status as they are. A PR's CI runs on its head merged onto the `dev`
  of its last push, so two PRs each green against the same `dev` can be
  red together (#1425 and #1426, #1489 and #1497). The run on `dev` after
  the merge tests that combination, and a red there opens its own issue
  (`report-dev-red.yml`). One check does look at the combination when the
  label goes on, because the break costs every lane its pushes and takes
  seconds to see: the head must merge with the newest `dev`, and the fuzz
  lockfiles must agree with the workspace lock on that merge. It reads
  files and runs no test. A merge after the label is `dev`'s run's.
- **A stacked PR retargeted onto `dev` keeps its old base's checks until
  its next push.** Merge `dev` into it before it merges
  (`scripts/ci/check_pr_event_triggers.py` names why no event does this).
- **"Require branches to be up to date" is the alternative not taken.**
  It tests each merge result before it lands by making every author merge
  `dev` and rerun CI after every other merge; with the Rust job over an
  hour and six lanes open, each merge would cost the other five that run.
  The runs on `dev` test every combination once, after the merge; what
  lands before it is seen is a break between two changes each green alone.
- **No merge queue (rule 21).** A queue also tests each merge result
  before it lands, but a group passes only if every required check
  finishes inside the queue's timeout, and a group removed for a timeout
  or a conflict sends its PRs back to wait again: in the queue's last
  eight hours on `dev` (to 2026-11-07), one PR merged. Reopen when the required checks
  finish well inside a queue timeout (the Rust suite partitioned across
  runners is the step that gets there), and record what the queue would
  replace here.
